<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol conquest of the Song dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1235">1235</a>–<a href="/years/1279">1279</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Mongol campaigns against the Song · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> was a long series of campaigns from 1235 to 1279. Song resistance was strong, with river defences and fortified cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a> holding out for years. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> finally completed the conquest in 1279, uniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-conquest-of-the-song-dynasty.jpg" alt="Mongol conquest of the Song dynasty" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_Song_Wars.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>At his death in 1227 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> left a plan to strike the Jin capital by passing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> territory, and that spring a small Mongol force ravaged five prefectures of the Song Lizhou Circuit, the first armed clash between the two. In 1233 the Song allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and Song troops under Meng Gong helped destroy the Jin at Caizhou. A year later Song armies advanced as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> to take the old capitals, but were repelled by the Mongol garrisons under Tachir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From 1235 Köden attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> plain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a>, the gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> plain, capitulated in 1236, but Meng Gong retook it in 1239, and the governor Yu Jie fortified mountain strongholds such as Diaoyucheng and held Sichuan for ten more years. Terrain unsuited to horses, new diseases and the need for naval warfare made this one of the most difficult wars of the Mongol conquests.</p><p>The attacks intensified after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> in 1251. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> conquered the Kingdom of Dali in 1253, and Uriyangkhadai took the Đại Việt capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a> in January 1258 before marching north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>. Möngke entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> in 1258 with two-thirds of the Mongol strength but died near the Diaoyu Fortress on 11 August 1259, and Kublai, elected Great Khan in 1260, first had to fight his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toluid-civil-war">Toluid Civil War</a>.</p><p>The strongholds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> loyalists fell one by one, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> in 1276, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> in 1277 and Hezhou in 1279, and Wen Tianxiang was captured in February 1279. The war ended at Yamen on 19 March 1279, where 1,000 Song warships, roped together in a solid mass, could neither attack, manoeuvre nor escape when the Yuan fleet of Zhang Hongfan and Li Heng closed in.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The conquest is considered the last great military achievement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>: with it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> ruled all of continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. Cities that surrendered, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>, were spared sacking, defecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> troops formed the Newly Submitted Army and received land, and many Tusi chiefdoms of the south-west kept their integrity as Yuan vassals. Song officers and officials fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa">Champa</a>, and former Song soldiers served in the army Trần Thánh Tông prepared against the second Mongol invasion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol conquest of the Song dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
